Tiananmen Square this morning no sign of  an anniversary instead unprecedented  security and certainly no chance of us  stopping to ask anyone their reflections  on it all  it was a pivotal moment for China and  the consequences run deep even today  there's no figure for the number who  died on June the 4th 1989 hundreds  certainly some say thousands a peaceful  push for democracy was halted by  communist leaders determined to hold on  to power the defining moment at least  for those watching the pictures beamed  around the world was this one man's last  stand 25 years on that image and the  moment it represents has been entirely  airbrushed from history within China  what happened right here and just down  there in Tiananmen Square simply isn't  discussed the subject is so sensitive  we're not allowed to film anywhere near  the square activists academics even  relatives of those who died are being  detained almost daily and so to hear the  voices of those who are allowed to speak  and to protest you must travel a  thousand miles south to Hong Kong here  with relative political freedom they can  march to remember  many of those who survived escaped here  it was then a British colony  Kenneth lamb is a Hong Kong lawyer I was  stole startled then the whole street was  blood a quarter of a century ago he was  with the students in Tiananmen Square I  don't know how many of them was to  survive but I knew many of them thought  that a new Hong Kong Museum today's  generation of students who live outside  China learn what happened but  intriguingly among the visitors we did  find some from mainland China they  wouldn't talk but we're clearly  absorbing a version of history  unfamiliar to them Lee chung-yong  founded the museum in 89 he was in the  square he now wonders what China might  be like today if the protestors had not  been stopped and I can imagine that if  there's democracy in 25 years ago and  there will still be economic growth  there will be more better distribution  of wealth and there can be freedom  people would be happier  China's transformation these past 25  years is unquestionable cars now clog  the roads once claimed by the tanks the  Communist Party remains in power no one  mentions June the 4th and tank man to  this day his name in his fate remain a  mystery
 

